AI Clinical Trial Card: Large Language Models, Small Labor Market Effects

Authors: Anders Humlum, Emilie Vestergaard (University of Chicago / University of Copenhagen)

AI Model Details

Provider:

Multiple providers, including OpenAI

Model Name and Version:

Large Language Models such as ChatGPT

No single model, version, or configuration studied

Access Method:

Workers accessed LLMs through publicly available tools (e.g., ChatGPT and similar systems)

No centralized platform, interface, or experimental control over usage

Fine-tuning:

None reported

Models were used in standard, off-the-shelf form

Prompting / Configuration Parameters:

Fully user-driven

No control or observation of prompt design, temperature, or usage patterns

Performance on Standard Benchmarks:

Not reported

Human Participant Profile

Sample Size:

Large-scale administrative and survey data covering many workers

Exact sample sizes vary by dataset and specification

Population / Expertise:

Workers across a broad range of occupations in the labor market

Includes occupations considered highly exposed to LLMs (e.g., writing-intensive or analytical roles)

Domain Expertise:

Varies substantially across occupations

AI / LLM Familiarity:

Increasing over the study period as LLMs diffused

Measured indirectly via task exposure and survey evidence

Training / Onboarding:

None

AI adoption occurred organically rather than through structured rollout

Interaction and Task Design

Task Description:

Not a task-level experiment

Study examines real-world adoption of LLMs and downstream labor market outcomes

Interface:

Self-selected by users

Includes chat interfaces and other publicly accessible AI tools

AI Role / Degree of Autonomy:

Assistive

AI systems supported task execution but did not autonomously perform jobs

Experimental / Identification Strategy:

Quasi-experimental design exploiting:

Differences in occupational exposure to LLM capabilities

Timing of LLM diffusion across the economy

Primary Outcome Measures:

Wages

Hours worked

Employment levels

Main Effects:

Despite rapid adoption of LLMs:

No large or immediate effects on wages, employment, or hours worked

Even in highly exposed occupations, estimated effects are small

Notes on Interpretation

This paper differs fundamentally from others in your set:

Focuses on aggregate labor market outcomes, not task-level performance

Lacks control over model configuration, interface, or usage intensity

The null or small effects likely reflect:

Partial adoption

Complementarity rather than substitution

Adjustment frictions in labor markets

Highlights a key distinction between:

Micro-level productivity gains observed in controlled experiments

Macro-level labor market effects, which may emerge slowly or not at all